      "content": "That's a fascinating direction to take. By treating the spikes as the underlying signal, you're essentially looking for a model that can capture the temporal dynamics of these probability shifts. Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) or long short-term memory (LSTM) networks could be a good fit for modeling the temporal relationships between the spikes. You can use techniques like time series forecasting or sequence-to-sequence models to predict the timing of the next spike, given the frequency and magnitude of previous spikes. Another approach is to use techniques from chaos theory and complexity science, such as phase space reconstruction or Lyapunov exponent analysis, to model the underlying dynamics of the probability shifts.",
